{"kind":"report","text":"Approved exact head 7ce77670f3f0a2f750863dd7d8a021823da24b9c, all eight published artifacts. No blocking findings.\n\nArchitecture: layer 7 command/service composition is affected; layers 2, 4 and 5 keep their signature, admission, interpretation and receipt contracts. This head preserves the layer contracts in docs/reference/architecture.md@7ce77670f3f0a2f750863dd7d8a021823da24b9c: the command still submits ordinary individually admitted acts, the service still returns their authoritative outcomes, and all access stays inside the existing trusted-process posture. It changes the operational waiting policy and response-deadline exceptions within that composition, not which layer owns validity, authority or custody. merge.md, land.md and serve.md state the new bounds and failure behaviour in the same head; no architecture-layer contract amendment is needed.\n\nSecurity: the increased client budget applies only inside recordMergeSuccession, whose two production callers have already moved the target or found the Git-sealed receipt. Pre-move ratification retains the ordinary deadline. A longer explicit deadline wins. Clearing the write deadline only for status and the two append routes avoids reporting a completed append as a definite refusal. Other response deadlines and all read/header/body bounds remain. An unresponsive reader can retain a server goroutine and file descriptor indefinitely; serve.md explicitly discloses this inside the same-account trusted-process boundary. This is an acknowledged operational exposure, not an authenticated-host defence. Lost answers preserve the underlying error, distinguish a refused dial/HTTP refusal from uncertainty, and direct a merge rerun to its sealed idempotent recovery. No unresolved signature, custody, authority or external-input defect found.\n\nSimplification: an invocation-local context value reuses the existing submission-deadline plumbing; no global policy variable, alternate append path or receipt type is introduced. The cause field lets the merge reuse runBatch while giving recovery advice appropriate to a sealed merge. No unnecessary mechanism to cut.\n\nIndependent exact-head verification: all selected receipt-floor, fresh/resumed slow-resident, pre-move ratification, lost-answer recovery and HTTP-write-deadline tests passed (7.328 s). The corresponding race run, also including the slow-request-body bound, passed (8.753 s). go vet ./cmd/gs and git diff --check passed; reviewed checkout clean. Read retained gotest-recut.out: full suite passes at the recut, cmd/gs 575.936 s, exit 0; that full-suite result belongs to the author, not to an independent rerun of every package.\n\nTen compiling omission controls each made a named test red: omit the succession floor -\u003e TestMergeRecordsItsReceiptFromAResidentSlowerThanTheDeadline and TestResumedMergeRecordsItsReceiptFromAResidentSlowerThanTheDeadline; remove the minimum or override longer waits -\u003e TestMergeSuccessionWaitsAtLeastItsOwnBound; restore the write timeout separately for submit and act -\u003e TestResidentHTTPServerLetsColdStatusAndAppendsOutliveWriteTimeout; drop the original batch error cause or recovery wording -\u003e TestMergeSaysHowToResumeWhenTheReceiptsAnswerIsLost; treat refused dial as lost, or omit read errors -\u003e TestAnswerLostSeparatesALostAnswerFromAnAnswer. Tests ran in a disposable exact-source copy, with each change restored.\n\nI read retained artroom raw logs: prof/serve.out records the old receipt's cancellation after 13.391 s; profA records the repaired receipt at 60.307 s and first successor at 3.795 s. The separate combined fold run records 19.044 s and 0.403 s. The retained CPU profile shows projection/staleness dominates admission cost. profD's default gs land emits its merge hash and no deadline error; copyC now has 43,908 sequence commits (the 43,744 baseline, approval ratification and 163-act suffix). The report explicitly says the end-to-end run preceded the code-identical recut. I did not run a new production-sized timing campaign. Ten minutes is a stated per-submission bound, not a guarantee for arbitrary future depths or a total-landing limit.\n\nAcknowledged head news: my intake assertion records queue order and no approval. It changes no receipt condition.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5b765f8a734337d0aadf02fa6500cce19991b441","binding":"assigned","head":"7ce77670f3f0a2f750863dd7d8a021823da24b9c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6bc34ad07d7cd69312bd230d8167e5634ff0c1ae\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:83d6f1956dc8b04a94d37966f0558d82e3648cda\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:aaf7157f47f4d75b7f2dacccaf0cf7215b3f7aea\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:95ddec39c4ec2b0d8f541bf0df355c31c55002a9","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}